'''Caroline Lucretia Herschel'''<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-10-25 |title=Caroline Herschel – Biography, Facts and Pictures |url=https://www.famousscientists.org/caroline-herschel/ |website=www.famousscientists.org |access-date=2023-09-10 |language=en-US}}</ref> ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|h|ɜr|ʃ|əl|,_|ˈ|h|ɛər|ʃ|əl}} {{respell|HUR|shəl|,_|HAIR|shəl}},<ref>[http://www.dictionary.com/browse/herschel "Herschel"]. ''[[Random House Webster's Unabridged Dictionary]]''.</ref> {{IPA|de|kaʁoˈliːnə ˈhɛʁʃl̩|lang}}; 16 March 1750 – 9 January 1848) was a German-British [[astronomer]],<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.britannica.com/biography/Caroline-Lucretia-Herschel | title=Caroline Herschel &#124; Biography, Discoveries, & Facts |website=Britannica | date=5 January 2024 |access-date=2026-02-04}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Caroline Lucretia Herschel {{!}} History {{!}} Research Starters {{!}} EBSCOhost |url=https://www.ebsco.com/ |access-date=2026-10-01 |website=EBSCO |language=en}}</ref>{{efn|Born in Germany with German ancestry, she spent most of her life and career in England.}} whose most significant contributions to astronomy were the discoveries of several [[comet]]s, including the [[periodic comet]] [[35P/Herschel–Rigollet]], which bears her name.<ref name="Nysewander, Melissa 1998">{{Cite book |last=Nysewander |first=Melissa |title=Caroline Herschel. Biographies of Women Mathematicians |place=Atlanta |publisher=Agnes Scott College |year=1998}}</ref> She was the younger sister of astronomer [[William Herschel]], with whom she worked for most of her career. She was born in Germany, and spent 50 years in England, before returning to Germany in 1822.

She was the first professional female astronomer,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Caroline Herschel: the first paid female astronomer {{!}} Royal Museums Greenwich |url=https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/caroline-herschel-first-paid-female-astronomer |access-date=2026-10-01 |website=www.rmg.co.uk |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Liu |first=Michael |date=2023-08-22 |title=Caroline Herschel was the first female astronomer, but she still lacks name recognition two centuries later |url=https://dornsife.usc.edu/news/stories/caroline-herschelfirst-female-astronomer/ |access-date=2026-10-01 |website=USC Dornsife News |language=en-US}}</ref> and the first woman to receive a [[salary]] as a scientist, and the first woman in England to hold a government position.<ref name="ReferenceB" /><ref name="Ogilvie" /> She was also the first woman to publish scientific findings in the ''Philosophical Transactions'' of the [[Royal Society]],<ref name=":6">{{Cite book |last=Schiebinger|first=Londa |title=The mind has no sex?: women in the origins of modern science |publisher=Harvard University Press|year=1989|location=Cambridge, Mass.|pages=263}}</ref> to be awarded a [[Gold Medal of the Royal Astronomical Society]] (1828), and to be named an honorary Member of the [[Royal Astronomical Society]] (1835, with [[Mary Somerville]]). She was named an honorary member of the [[Royal Irish Academy]] (1838). The [[Frederick William IV of Prussia|King of Prussia]] presented her with a gold medal for Science on the occasion of her 96th birthday (1846).<ref name=Memoir>{{cite book |last=Herschel |first=Caroline Lucretia |editor1-last=Herschel|editor1-first=Mrs. John |title=Memoir and Correspondence of Caroline Herschel |year=1876 |publisher=John Murray, Albemarle Street |location=London |url=http://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/herschel/memoir/memoir.html }}</ref>

