{{Jews and Judaism sidebar |expanded=population}}

The '''history of the Jews in Russia''' goes back to the beginnings of the [[Russia|Russian state]]. At one time, the [[Russian Empire]] hosted the largest population of [[Jewish diaspora|Jews in the world]].<ref>{{cite encyclopedia|url=http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/table.jsp?table_id=427&volid=11&title=STATISTICS|title=Table of Ratios of Jewish to Total Population in the Principal Countries and Cities of the World|encyclopedia=[[Jewish Encyclopedia]]|date=1901–1906|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070328175501/http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/table.jsp?table_id=427&volid=11&title=STATISTICS|archive-date=March 28, 2007|access-date=September 13, 2016}}</ref> Within these territories, the primarily [[Ashkenazi Jews|Ashkenazi Jewish]] communities of many different areas flourished and developed many of modern [[Judaism]]'s most distinctive theological and cultural traditions, and they also faced periods of [[antisemitism|antisemitic]] [[Antisemitism in Russia|discriminatory policies and persecution]], including violent [[pogrom]]s.

Many analysts have documented a "renaissance" in the Jewish community inside Russia since the beginning of the 21st century;<ref name="Chicago Tribune">[https://www.chicagotribune.com/2001/11/23/renaissance-of-jewish-life-in-russia/ Renaissance of Jewish life in Russia]  November 23, 2001, By John Daniszewski, Chicago Tribune</ref> however, the Russian Jewish population has experienced precipitous decline since the [[dissolution of the USSR]] which continues to this day, although it is still [[History of the Jews in Europe|among the largest in Europe]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.pewforum.org/2012/12/18/global-religious-landscape-jew/|title=Jews|work=Pew Research Center|date=18 December 2012|access-date=17 March 2021|archive-date=August 5, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130805050438/https://www.pewforum.org/2012/12/18/global-religious-landscape-jew/|url-status=live}}</ref>

