A '''prelude''' ({{langx|de|Präludium}} or ''{{lang|de|Vorspiel}}''; {{langx|la|praeludium}}; {{langx|fr|prélude}}; {{langx|it|preludio}}; {{Langx|ru|прелюдия}}) is a short [[Musical piece|piece]] of [[music]], the [[musical form|form]] of which may vary from piece to piece.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thefreedictionary.com/preludes|title=preludes|access-date=9 April 2018|via=The Free Dictionary}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/474848/prelude|title=Prelude - music|website=britannica.com|access-date=9 April 2018}}</ref> While, during the [[Baroque era]], for example, it may have served as an introduction to succeeding movements of a work that were usually longer and more complex, it may also have been a stand-alone piece of work during the [[Romantic era]]. It generally features a small number of rhythmic and melodic [[Motif (music)|motif]]s that recur through the piece. Stylistically, the prelude is improvisatory in nature. The term may also refer to an [[overture]], particularly to an [[opera]] or an [[oratorio]].

A '''prelude and fugue''' generally consists of two [[Movement (music)|movements]] in the same key for solo [[Keyboard music|keyboard]]. In [[classical music]], the combination of [[Prelude (music)|prelude]] and [[fugue]] is one with a long history. Many composers have written works of this kind. The use of this format is generally inspired by [[Johann Sebastian Bach]]'s two books of preludes and fugues, ''[[The Well-Tempered Clavier]],'' completed in 1722 and 1742 respectively. Bach, however, was not the first to compose such a set: [[Johann Caspar Ferdinand Fischer]] wrote a 20-key cycle in his 1702 work ''[[Ariadne musica]]''.

Many composers have written sets of 24 Preludes, such as [[The Well-Tempered Clavier|Bach]], [[Preludes (Chopin)|Chopin]], [[24 Preludes, Op. 11 (Scriabin)|Scriabin]], [[24 Preludes and Fugues (Shostakovich)|Shostakovich]], and [[Préludes (Debussy)|Debussy]].

{{Short description|Harmonic grouping of notes}}
{{Redirect|Chording|the computer input method|Chorded keyboard}}
{{Redirect|Akkord|the corp|Akkord Industry Construction Investment Corporation}}

[[File:Major chord on C.png|thumb|A C major chord.]]
A '''chord''' is the simultaneous sound of two or more [[musical note]]s. The most common chord has three notes and is known as a [[Triad (music)|triad]]. [[Added tone chord]]s, [[extended chord]]s, and [[tone cluster]]s can have more than three notes and are common in [[contemporary classical music]] and [[jazz]].

An [[arpeggio]] is a chord where notes are sounded separately. A series of chords is sometimes called a [[chord progression]]. There are several ways to notate chords including [[figured bass]], [[Roman numeral analysis|Roman numerals]], the [[Nashville Number System]], and alphabetical [[chord notation]].

