'''''National Geographic''''' (formerly '''''The National Geographic Magazine''''',<ref name="first-name"/> sometimes branded as '''''Nat Geo''''')<ref name="NatGeo"/> is an American monthly magazine published by [[National Geographic Partners]] and is based in [[Washington, DC]].<ref name="partners"/> <!-- History -->The magazine was founded in 1888 as a scholarly journal, nine months after the establishment of the society, but is now a popular magazine. In 1905, it began including pictures, a style for which it became well known. Its first color photos appeared in the 1910s. During the [[Cold War]], the magazine committed itself to presenting a balanced view of the [[physical geography|physical]] and [[human geography]] of countries beyond the [[Iron Curtain]]. Later, the magazine became outspoken on [[environmental issues]].

Until 2015, the magazine was completely owned and managed by the [[National Geographic Society]]. Since 2015, [[controlling interest]] has been held by National Geographic Partners. <!-- Format -->Topics of features generally concern [[geography]], [[history]], [[nature]], [[science]], and [[world culture]]. The magazine is well known for its distinctive appearance: a thick [[Bookbinding#Thermally activated binding|square-bound]] glossy format with a yellow rectangular border. Map supplements from [[National Geographic Maps]] are included with subscriptions, and it is available in a traditional printed edition and an interactive online edition.

<!-- Circulation and awards -->{{As of|1995}}, the magazine was circulated worldwide in nearly forty local-language editions and had a global circulation of at least 6.5 million per month including 3.5 million within the U.S.,<ref name="fox-deal-1"/><ref name="fox-deal-2"/> down from about 12 million in the late 1980s. {{As of|2015}}, the magazine had won 25 [[National Magazine Awards]].<ref name="awards"/> In 2023, ''National Geographic'' laid off all staff writers and announced they would stop U.S. newsstand sales in the next year.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nair |first=Deepthi |date=2023-06-29 |title=National Geographic lays off all staff writers and will stop US newsstand sales |url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/2023/06/29/national-geographic-lays-off-all-staff-writers-and-will-stop-us-newsstand-sales/ |access-date=2023-07-10 |website=The National |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Clayton |first=Abené |date=2023-06-29 |title=National Geographic reportedly lays off its last staff writers |language=en-GB |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2023/jun/28/national-geographic-staff-layoffs-report |access-date=2023-07-10 |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=My new National Geographic just arrived, which includes my latest feature—my 16th, and my last as a senior writer. NatGeo is laying off all of its staff writers. I've been so lucky. I got to work w/incredible journalists and tell important, global stories. It's been an honor. |url=https://twitter.com/CraigAWelch/status/1674038159322275841 |access-date=2023-07-10 |website=Twitter |language=en}}</ref> {{As of|2024|11}}, its [[Instagram]] page has 280 million followers, the [[List of most-followed Instagram accounts|third most]] of any account not belonging to an individual celebrity.<ref name="insta"/> The magazine's combined U.S. and international circulation as of June 30, 2024, was about 1.65 million, with its children's magazines separately achieving a circulation of about 500,000.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Magazine media – Circulation averages |url=https://abcas3.auditedmedia.com/ecirc/magtitlesearch.asp |access-date=August 20, 2024 |website=Alliance for Audited Media}}</ref>

